Article27a
Access to energy, electricity networks and industrial self-consumption for industrial acceleration areas
1. Before an industrial acceleration area is designated or a strategic project is approved, the competent authorities shall carry out an energy and network assessment covering at least:
(a) the available electricity network capacity;
(b) any reinforcement required to the transmission and distribution networks;
(c) binding time limits for connection to the electricity network;
(d) the feasibility of individual and collective industrial self-consumption;
(e) access to shared energy storage facilities;
(f) the availability of bilateral renewable power purchase agreements for industrial undertakings;
(g) the establishment of industrial energy communities within industrial parks;
(h) coordination arrangements between the competent public authorities, transmission and distribution system operators, electricity suppliers and industrial operators; and
(i) the appointment of a regional energy coordinator, who shall cooperate with the coordinator responsible for strategic projects.
2. Member States shall ensure that projects located in industrial acceleration areas benefit from a dedicated and streamlined procedure for connection to the electricity network; individual and collective self-consumption installations; and energy storage facilities, including battery storage.

When carrying out its review, the Commission shall pay particular attention to the following:
(a) the effectiveness and impact of this Regulation on competitiveness, administrative burden and procurement costs;
(b) the persistence of the circumstances that have justified the adoption of this Regulation;
(c) the proportionality of Union origin and low-carbon requirements for products from certain sectors as defined in this Regulation;
(d) the necessity to introduce Union origin and/or low-carbon requirements for products from certain sectors critical to the Union’s economic security, including but not limited to the building of ships, rail rolling stock, fertilizers and aviation sector;
(e) the proportionality of the minimum percentage shares of low-carbon materials and products in Annex II;
(f) the availability and sufficiency of supply of the battery components in the Union covered by Annex III;
(g) the necessity to amend or extend the scope of Annexes II and III to additional products from the energy-intensive industries listed in Annex I, or to additional strategic sectors not currently covered.

When carrying out its review, the Commission shall pay particular attention to:
(a) the effectiveness of this Regulation and the persistence of the circumstances that have justified its adoption;
(b) the necessity to introduce Union origin requirements for products from certain sectors critical to the Union's economic security, notably the building of ships and of rail rolling stock;
(c) the availability and sufficiency of EU-manufactured supply of all components and materials subject to the 'Made in Europe' requirements set out in Annexes II and III, assessed against the thresholds applicable at the time of the review;
(d) where the review finds that EU supply of one or more required components or materials is insufficient to enable compliance with the applicable requirements without disproportionate cost or delay, the Commission shall, as part of the same review, propose targeted adjustments to the relevant thresholds or timelines by means of a delegated act adopted in accordance with Article 30, or, where appropriate, through a legislative proposal.

